{
  "config_version": "given_names_gender_v1",
  "purpose": "Gender implied by a common Egyptian given name, used by the name_implied_gender_vs_digit_13 cross-check to detect a misread digit 13. Detection only: this file may never drive a repair of any digit.",
  "policy": [
    "A name that is not in this file yields not_available. The check never guesses, and never infers gender from a name's ending: normalize_arabic folds teh marbuta to heh, so the usual 'ends in a taa marbuta means female' rule does not even survive normalization.",
    "Only names whose gender is unambiguous in Egyptian usage are listed. Anything used for both sexes is listed under deliberately_excluded_ambiguous and stays not_available forever, because a false flag in a government system accuses a citizen.",
    "Names are matched in the comparison form produced by text_norm.normalize_arabic, so hamza, alef, alef maksura and teh marbuta variants all match without separate entries.",
    "A name appearing in both lists is a configuration error and is rejected at load time rather than silently resolved.",
    "This list is configuration, not code. Extending it is a reviewable data change; each entry carries a romanization so a reviewer who does not read Arabic can audit it."
  ],
  "coverage_note": "The list is deliberately partial. Its value is the cards it can speak for; on every other card the check abstains. Coverage and false-flag rate are measured and reported, never assumed.",
  "male_prefix_tokens": [
    {"ar": "عبد", "en": "Abd", "why": "Arabic construct 'servant of' + a divine name. Grammatically masculine and never a female given name, so a first token of 'عبد' implies male on its own. This is a rule of the language, not an inference from frequency."}
  ],
